Consider binary logistic regression with labels $y_i \in \{-1, +1\}$. With the bias absorbed into $\mathbf{w}$, the model assumes

$$
\begin{aligned}
P(y_i = +1 \mid \mathbf{x}_i ; \mathbf{w}) &= \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\mathbf{w}^T\mathbf{x}_i}} \\[0.6em]
P(y_i = -1 \mid \mathbf{x}_i ; \mathbf{w}) &= \frac{1}{1 + e^{\mathbf{w}^T\mathbf{x}_i}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Given a dataset $D = \{(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}$, the MLE is $\hat{\mathbf{w}} = \arg\max_{\mathbf{w}} P(D ; \mathbf{w})$. Fill in the blanks to derive its final form.

Because the samples are assumed to be $\blank{1}$, the likelihood of the whole dataset can be written in terms of the individual samples.

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\log P(D ; \mathbf{w}) \\
&= \log \blank{2} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log P(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i ; \mathbf{w}) \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log \Big[ \, \blank{3} \, \Big] \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log P(y_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i ; \mathbf{w}) \; + \; \blank{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

The term $\blank{4}$ does not depend on $\mathbf{w}$, so it is dropped. Since $y_i \in \{-1, +1\}$, the two probabilities above can be combined into a single expression in $y_i$:

$$
P(y_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i ; \mathbf{w}) = \blank{5}
$$

Substituting this expression gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathbf{w}}
&= \arg\max_{\mathbf{w}} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log \blank{5} \\
&= \arg\max_{\mathbf{w}} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Big[ \log 1 \; - \; \blank{6} \, \Big] \\
&= \blank{7}_{\,\mathbf{w}} \; \sum_{i=1}^{n} \blank{6}
\end{aligned}
$$

For MAP, assume a zero-mean Gaussian prior written as $P(\mathbf w)\propto\exp(-\lambda\mathbf w^T\mathbf w)$ with $\lambda>0$. After taking the negative logarithm and dropping additive constants, the MAP estimate minimizes

$$
J_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\mathbf w)=\sum_{i=1}^n\log(1+e^{-y_i\mathbf w^T\mathbf x_i})+\blank{8}.
$$

A univariate logistic regression model for labels $y \in \{0, 1\}$ sets

$$
p(x) = P(y = 1 \mid x) = \frac{e^{w_1 x + w_0}}{1 + e^{w_1 x + w_0}}
$$

Fill in the blanks to find when class $1$ is at least as likely as class $0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
1 - p(x) &= \blank{1} \\[0.6em]
\frac{p(x)}{1 - p(x)} &= \blank{2} \\[0.6em]
\log \frac{p(x)}{1 - p(x)} &= \blank{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The ratio $\frac{p(x)}{1 - p(x)}$ is called the $\blank{4}$, and its logarithm is called the $\blank{5}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
&p(x) \ge 1 - p(x) \\[0.4em]
&\Leftrightarrow\; \frac{p(x)}{1 - p(x)} \ge \blank{6} \\[0.4em]
&\Leftrightarrow\; \blank{3} \ge \blank{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

On the decision boundary the two classes are equally likely, so $p(x) = \blank{8}$ there.

In logistic regression let $z = \mathbf{w}^T\mathbf{x} + b$. The model assigns the two classes the probabilities

$$
\begin{aligned}
P(\text{positive} \mid \mathbf{x}) &= \frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}} \\[0.6em]
P(\text{negative} \mid \mathbf{x}) &= \frac{1}{1 + e^{z}}
\end{aligned}
$$

(a) Show that these two probabilities add up to $1$.

(b) Encode the positive class as $y = +1$ and the negative class as $y = -1$. Write a single formula for $P(y \mid \mathbf{x})$ that covers both classes, and verify it by substituting $y = +1$ and $y = -1$.

(c) Suppose the negative class were encoded as $y = 0$ instead. What value does your formula from (b) give for $y = 0$, and why does this show that the formula cannot be used with labels in $\{0, 1\}$?
